Brakka holds still in the shuttered dark and waits, and after a long moment the grey-green light appears again at the edge of the dark. It comes closer, low to the floor, swinging a little with each dragging step, and as it nears the edge of his vision it stops being a light and becomes a man.

It is a miner. Brakka can see the shape of him now: heavy boots, a pick over one shoulder, a lamp in his hand burning with that cold grey-green flame that gives no warmth and hardly any glow beyond itself. He walks wrong. His head lolls to one side as though the neck will no longer hold it, and one leg drags behind him, the toe scraping stone with every step. His face, when the strange light catches it, is the colour of old wax, and his eyes are open and fixed on nothing.

Then a buckle on Brakka's mail rasps against his shield, a small sound in the huge quiet, and the dead man's head jerks up. The fixed eyes find him. The dragging step quickens, and the thing comes shambling up the drift toward him, lamp swinging, pick still across its shoulder, and halts ten feet away, swaying, as though something inside it is deciding whether he is meat or not.

