Brakka sets his feet and draws one long, steadying breath, and the worst of the hurt settles out of him; the ringing in his ribs dulls to a deep ache. Then he stops waiting for the thing's next swing and meets it.

The longsword takes the dead miner's neck square, and the strength of it carries clean through. The head comes away and strikes the floor with a sound like a dropped stone, and the body stands a moment longer, arms out, as though it has not yet understood — and then it folds, all at once, and the cold green lamp falls from its hand and rolls, its flame sputtering against the wet stone until it dies and leaves only Brakka's own yellow light, and the smell of cold earth and wrong sweetness.

In the sudden quiet, the drift holds its breath with him. He looks down at the face in the lamplight, and knows it.

Orrin. A young dwarf, a miner, not long out of his apprenticeship — Brakka has passed him a hundred times at the temple gate and in the drifthead crowd at shift's end, a quiet lad who kept his own counsel and never shirked a hard seam. His eyes are open now, and empty, and whatever walked in his body is gone out of it.

The way down is open. Beyond the fallen lamp, the drift runs on into the dark, and the other two sets of boot-prints lead deeper.

