Brakka kneels, gathers up Orrin's severed head, and eases its eyes shut with the flat of his thumb, the way he has done for the temple's dead. The lids are cold and will not stay at first, and he presses them until they do. Then he bows his head and speaks the old words over the boy — not the grand prayers of the rites, but the small one a miner says for a comrade gone into the stone, asking the Anvil Father to take back what was His, and to weigh the spent years kindly. His voice sounds very small in the drift, and the dark takes it.

Then he looks the body over.

The killing wound is under the ruined shirt: three long tears across the chest, parallel as a rake, clawed open to the ribs. The flesh is grey and dry, and the wounds gape without a drop of blood, as though whatever opened him did it after his heart had stopped, or drank him dry as it went. His fingertips tell the rest. The nails are split and the pads worn raw, crusted with rock dust — Orrin clawed at the stone, in the dark, for a long time before he died. He did not fall and die quickly. He was down here, alive, scraping at the walls, and something came for him in the end.

Around the body's neck is a leather cord with a little iron hammer charm, a token of the Anvil Father, gone cold as the rest of him. Brakka leaves it where it lies. There is nothing else of use on the body but the miner's pick, still lying where it fell beside the dead lamp.

