Brakka leaves the hammer charm with Orrin, but he takes the miner's pick and the cold green lamp, and draws the dead man's cloak up over his face so the town can recover him properly when this is done. Then he turns to the timber nearest the body and cuts a deep cross into it with the edge of his sword — a waymark, since the rope has spent all its length and will carry him no further. He resolves to cut another at every turning, so he can find his way back in the dark.

Shield up, he follows the two remaining sets of boot-prints deeper.

The drift runs down for a long while, and the air grows heavier and wetter, the walls running with moisture that glistens in the lantern light. Here and there he cuts his cross into a timber. The two sets of prints keep close together, never straying, as though the two men walked side by side the whole way. The timbers overhead grow fewer, and the stone shows through — raw, ancient rock, cold to the air.

At last the drift opens into a wider space, a low stope where the old miners once worked a seam, the walls scarred with pick-work and the floor littered with broken rock. Here the two sets of prints hesitate and circle, as though the men stopped. Against one wall is the mark of a small camp: a cold scatter of burnt timber, two packs slumped against the stone, a single boot left behind. And the prints leave the camp and cross the stope to a narrow passage in the far wall, where the two of them go in one behind the other.

Somewhere beyond that passage, water drips, slow and even, like a pulse.

