Brakka searches the camp in the lantern light.

The first pack has a name cut into the strap, old and worn into the leather: Davor. Inside are the common things a miner carries — a twist of hard rations, a waterskin, a spare pick handle, a whetstone — and, folded in a scrap of oilcloth against the damp, a sheet of cheap paper. On it, in charcoal, is a rough map of the old workings: the drift he has walked, the stope, the narrow passage ahead, and a way deeper still, all the way to a mark drawn dark and deliberate — an X — beside which a single word is written. "Pocket."

The second pack is Osrik's, by the same rough marking. It holds more rations, a tin of lamp oil, a coil of thin cord, and a little iron hammer charm of the Anvil Father, twin to the one Orrin wore, still on its cord. A half-eaten loaf of hard bread lies beside it, as though the man set it down and never picked it up again.

The boot by the wall is a single right boot, its laces snapped and its sole worn through. It sits apart from the camp, pointed toward the passage, dropped in a hurry — a man who pulled free of it and ran.

The ashes are cold and dead, days old. Among them are the ends of two split sticks, and in the dust the flattened places where two men sat close, back to back, before they went on into the narrow passage.

The map's X is deeper than any drift he has yet walked.

