Brakka shuts the lantern to a slit and sinks behind a tumble of broken stone at the gallery's mouth, and in the near-dark he watches, and the grey-green light teaches him.

It is one walker, and the light is not held in its hand. Brakka is sure of that now. The cold green flame hangs in the air low above the ground just ahead of the dead miner, and it does not swing with the body's steps as a carried lamp would. It drifts a little, now ahead, now behind, now darting a yard to the side as though it were looking about on its own. The corpse below it shambles in a slow, steady circuit along the foot of the great stepped wall — back and forth, back and forth, before a dark opening in the rock. It is not wandering. It is pacing a watch.

And beyond the opening, deeper in that black passage, the very edge of a second grey-green light glimmers, faint as a will-o'-the-marsh, and is gone.

As Brakka watches, his knee shifts a loose stone, and it grates against the rubble with a sound like a match struck. The pacing stops. The corpse turns, slow as a door on a rusted hinge, and the green flame drifts out ahead of it, toward him, and holds there, regarding the gallery's mouth. Then the dead man starts toward him across the wide floor, one dragging step after another, and the light comes with it.

