The dead man closes the last of the distance and Brakka meets it at the passage mouth, where the narrow rock funnels it to him one on one. Its first swing he turns on the shield, and the second he answers — the longsword opens a wound across its chest that does not bleed, and still it presses in without a sound. Its next blow scrapes off the rim of the shield and falls away.

In the dim slit of lantern light the face is clear now at this range, and Brakka knows it as he knew the other. Osrik. An older dwarf, broad in the shoulder, who worked the deep seams and had a laugh you could hear across a whole gallery at shift's end. The laugh is gone, and the man is gone with it; what walks against him now wears Osrik's boots and Osrik's face and nothing else of him. In Brakka's belt-pouch the little iron hammer charm is cold and still.

The grey-green light that came with the dead man now hangs low and watchful just behind it, as though it is waiting to see how this ends.

It is Brakka's turn again.

