Brakka drives the longsword into the open neck with all his weight, and the blade sinks nearly to the spine. Osrik's knees fold, and he begins to go down at last — and then the body jerks, every limb at once, and sways upright again. The head lolls on the ruined neck, the wound gapes, and still the thing stands there, refusing the ground. Whatever holds it will not let go; it has died a death already, and it will not die again for a sword's persuasion.

The grey-green light darts in close, quick as a wasp, and hangs there shivering just over the corpse's shoulder, brightening, as though the near-fall has stirred it to anger or hunger.

It is Brakka's turn again, and Osrik will not stay down.

What does Brakka do?
