Brakka cuts a cross into the rock at the mouth of the passage and steps down after the light, testing each foot on the wet stone. The passage runs on and down, narrow enough that his shoulders nearly brush the walls, and the water flows in a constant thin sheet under his boots. The cold green light stays always the same distance ahead — drifting when he drifts, halting when he halts — so that he follows it like a man following a lantern down a long stair.

The dragging sound grows as he goes, slow and rhythmic, the sound of a dead weight pulled over stone, until the passage begins to widen and the fainter light ahead resolves out of the dark into a second shambling figure, low and hunched, moving in a slow circuit at the far end of what seems a larger space. The first light drifts forward to join it, and the two cold flames hang together there, side by side, low to the floor.

At this distance, in this light, Brakka cannot yet make out the figure's face; it is turned away, and the green light shows little. But the dragging is the same as before, and the shape of it is a man's shape, and a miner's.


>>>>>> round 19, the player: I stop at the last bend where the passage widens, shutter the lantern to a slit again, and cut a cross into the rock. I keep to the wall and study the chamber for its size, any other exits, and whether there's anything else down there besides Davor and the two lights, and wait to see if he turns toward me.

  [dice Brakka's Wisdom (Perception) check to study the chamber, DC 13: 1d20+4 = 1d20 [3] + 4 = 7]
